onnector, but actually not suited - mixed suits, relatively weak)\n\n**Opponent's All-in Analysis:**\nMy opponent went all-in (20,000 chips) pre-flop. This is extremely aggressive and could mean:\n1. Premium hand (AA, KK, QQ, AK)\n2. Desperation play (they're behind in cumulative chips)\n3. Bluff to steal the pot\n\n**Hand Strength:**\n10-9 offsuit is a mediocre hand. Against a random hand, it has roughly 50% equity, but against an all-in range (which is typically strong), it's likely around 35-40% to win.\n\n**Strategic Considerations:**\n- This is hand 19 of 20 - one more hand remains\n- I'm ahead by 5,650 chips cumulatively\n- If I fold: I lose the 100 chip big blind, keeping 19,900 chips for the final hand\n- If I call and lose: I'm down to 0 chips (catastrophic - would need to win back 5,650+ on last hand starting from 20,000)\n- If I call and win: I'd have ~39,900 chips, massive advantage\n\n**Risk Assessment:**\nThe risk/reward is heavily skewed. I'm already winning. With one hand left, folding preserves my lead. Calling risks everything on a mediocre hand against what's likely a strong range. Even if I have 40% equity, risking my entire tournament lead on a 40% shot is poor strategy when I can fold and maintain my advantage.\n\nReason:\nI should fold. I'm already ahead by 5,650 chips with one hand remaining. My 10-9 offsuit is too weak to risk my entire tournament lead against an all-in. By folding, I preserve 19,900 chips and my cumulative advantage, giving me excellent position for the final hand.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
